  "summary": "Ahead of a College Station City Council vote on renewing its Flock Safety contract, KBTX reviews findings from local group DeFlock BCS, which audited Flock records from College Station PD, Bryan PD, and Brazos County Sheriff's Office. Concerns include use of freeform/people searches, a Bryan search logged as 'immigration retention' (which Bryan says related to a protest threat investigation), millions of outside-agency searches of local data, camera security vulnerabilities, and that Flock-owned cameras remain in place after contract cancellation.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "In July of this year, College Station was using the object class people search to search for things like black backpack, blue pants",
      "paragraph_idx": 15
    },
    {
      "quote": "However, our network has been searched close to 3 million times. Because we share our data with a majority of places in Texas, anyone can search our data at any time.",
      "paragraph_idx": 20
    },
    {
      "quote": "The usage creep of the entire system has proven to be too fast for us to keep up with, too fast for policy to keep up with.",
      "paragraph_idx": 23
    },
    {
      "quote": "Our contract very clearly states that the Flock cameras are property of Flock. So even if a contract is canceled, we don't have the right to remove them.",
      "paragraph_idx": 29
    }
  ],
